  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=9 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-3(\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+3(\\frac{1}{3})=9\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=9+3-1=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\u27e811,3,\\frac{1}{3}\u27e9.$",
